<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Leine is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, flowing about 280 kilometres north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aller">Aller</a>. Its valley formed the heart of the Guelph principalities of Göttingen and Calenberg, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> held lands between it and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leine.jpg" alt="Leine" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruthe-Leine-Winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
